Doc Haven wrote:
> 
> I am quite familiar with the E-word. I still have not found what is
> most effective to write it with, as I sometimes have to write it with
> my finger because I have nothing else (a magic marker or something of
> that nature).

Best is burning with wands of fire or lightning; those engravings do not
erase, you'll get a permanent safe spot for your stash or for yourself.
There's just limited charges in the wands, though. Another excellent one
is an athame; it's not permanent but lasts long enough to fight from
that spot and re-engrave if necessary. Similar to the athame is a wand
of digging (or a magic marker), both with the drawback of limited charges.
All of the above require just a single turn to engrave that specific word.
Hard gems (or weapons, which become dull in the process) will engrave as
good as athames/wands of digging/magic markers, but they require one turn
per character in the word, and you may get attacked (or killed) while you
are writing. But you have the option to write a half of the word and add
append the second half after you finished writing; that way you can act
after four turns in case a monster approaches in the meantime. Finally
engraving in the dust with your fingers; it requires just one turn but is
least reliable, especially you cannot fight without erasing the engraving.

Just mind that some monsters (especially @) do not respect Elbereth.

> Poisoning is another problem that I have. What do you
> suggest towards that problem?

Well, there's an amulet and a ring that both grant poison resistance. But
unfortunately it's not that common to have got one early on in the game.
The usual source to obtain resistances is from eating monster corpses.
I suggest to consult one of the monster spoilers[*] for a list of those
monsters that give you poison resistance without being poisonous to you.
Some poisonous monsters confer the resistance as well, but the drawbacks
(lost strength, or even death) are usually not worth to risk that, at least
as long as you haven't found some means to immediately restore your lost
stats.

[*] Any list I could give you off the top of my head would be incomplete.

>> Especially if you're lacking food in the early game you shouldn't wait
>> too long for your pet to follow you.
> 
> That may actually be the first idea that I had when first thinking
> about leaving my pet behind... maybe that is a bad reason.

As long as your pet is nearby in the same room you typicall lose just a
few turns waiting, usually no problem, and it's worth to wait for your pet.
Occasionally the pets get kind of "trapped" in another room if you're
inattentive; then it depends on your food equipment and the role played
whether it's worth to go and get it. You may as well leave it behind and
come back later to get it; but there's some risk that it goes wild (i.e.
becomes untame) if you're absent for too long.

